   "subject": "Re: Measurement invariance: Examining configural fit for single factor with 3 items before examining other models",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"font-stretch:inherit;line-height:inherit;font-family:Arial,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:15px;vertical-align:baseline;clear:both;color:rgb(36,39,41)\">Can I say something about the fit from these results? How should I interpret these results and write about this in a report?</p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>No, the model is just-identified.  You have no extra information (df) with which to assess the fit of the configural model, but you can still test more constrained models in which parameters are equated.</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792418645069000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0M9ZnT_f8_rbpGQepBz5d-\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
