   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<br>So let me just check whether I understood this right.<div>I used the semTools::measurementInvariance() function - do I interpret these results as if there is metric invariance (because cfi.scaled is acceptable) or not (because the change in chi square is significant (there are coupe of thousands of participants) and delta cfi is 0.029)?</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you!</div><div><br></div><div> <img alt=\"Picture1.png\" width=\"621px\" height=\"257px\" src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/3c99ed1f8fd77/Picture1.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">Dana subota, 16. listopada 2021. u 15:31:43 UTC+2 korisnik Terrence Jorgensen napisao je:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>how can we tell whether metric invariance is achieved across several groups with just-identified models? </div></blockquote><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>They won&#39;t be just-identified when you impose metric-invariance constraints.  So yes, the change in chi-squared would simply be the chi-squared of the metric model.</div></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792418645072000&amp;usg=AOvVaw07aw_-OTVatsImvzXFSxf3\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
