  {
   "id": "SwHDaDNkBAAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2021-10-20T15:14:09Z",
   "subject": "Re: Measurement invariance: Examining configural fit for single factor with 3 items before examining other models",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>I used the semTools::measurementInvariance() function</div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>Don&#39;t.  It is based on outdated ideas that do not pan out in practice (e.g., testing scalar invariance when metric is already rejected; using change in a fit index as a complementary test or effect size).  There is a reason the function is deprecated.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> <br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>do I interpret these results as if there is metric invariance (because cfi.scaled is acceptable) or not (because the change in chi square is significant (there are coupe of thousands of participants) and delta cfi is 0.029)?</div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>I would definitely reject the null hypothesis of metric invariance.  With your large <i>N</i>, chi-squared certainly has a lot of power to detect small differences in measurement properties, but the most liberal criterion I have ever seen for change in CFI was &lt; 0.01 (which is as terrible an idea as any other such criterion; this is not what CFI was designed to tell you).  </div><div><br></div><div>With only 3 indicators, I think your only recourse is to fit 3 competing partial-metric-invariance models (each one freeing a different indicator&#39;s loading, while the other 2 are equated).  If any of them are statistically indistinguishable from configural-invariance fit (i.e., fail to reject H0), then you found your baseline to test partial scalar invariance.  But you might not find any pair of indicators whose loadings can be validly constrained to equality across groups, in which case the latent scales cannot be reliability linked across groups, preventing any meaningful comparison.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><br></div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen<br>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics<br>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam<br><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792418645073000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1caYodrUAj26GnIi2EoGAf\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a> <br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
